Project: Python Postgres/MySQL Data Generator

Goal
- Provide clear guardrails for maintaining and extending a small Python utility that
  generates sample transaction data in Postgres or MySQL and verifies connectivity.
- The MySQL path additionally supports a production-realistic DDL+DML simulation mode
  (schema drift, transfers, cascading deletes, soft deletes) built for a CDC ingestion
  PoC (Artie and Snowflake Openflow reading Aurora MySQL binlogs) — see "MySQL DDL/DML
  simulation" below. The Postgres path remains the original, simpler bulk-insert tool.

Tech stack and runtime
- Python 3.x (CLI scripts)
- Postgres via psycopg2 (including execute_values for batch inserts)
- MySQL via PyMySQL (executemany for batch inserts; no built-in connection pooling, so
  a small hand-rolled queue-based pool stands in for psycopg2.pool.SimpleConnectionPool)
- Optional: tqdm for progress bars
- pytest for tests; testcontainers[mysql] for dockerized MySQL integration tests
- requirements.txt pins psycopg2-binary, PyMySQL, tqdm, pytest, testcontainers[mysql].
  Install into a local venv/ (gitignored): python3 -m venv venv && ./venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt

Key files
- simulate_data.py: Postgres data generator. Creates/reset tables, inserts data
  sequentially or concurrently, and can simulate continuous production traffic.
- test_connection.py: Simple connectivity check that prints the Postgres version.
- cdcgen.py: convenience CLI wrapping the MySQL scripts (check/seed/simulate/reset/
  teardown/verify/selftest subcommands, kebab-case flags, defaults to
  python-datasource.json / CDC_POC_MYSQL). A thin subprocess dispatcher, not new
  logic — the scripts below remain the source of truth and stay directly runnable
  for anything cdcgen.py doesn't expose. See `./cdcgen.py --help` / `<subcommand> --help`.
- simulate_data_mysql.py: MySQL CLI entrypoint — bulk-seed phase plus the DDL+DML
  --simulate mode. Orchestration only; the actual logic lives in mysql_generator/.
- test_connection_mysql.py: MySQL connectivity check; also reports writer/reader role.
- mysql_generator/: package holding all MySQL-specific logic (see below).
- verify_snowflake.py + snowflake_verify/: separate tool that diffs Snowflake state
  against the generator's changelog (see "Verification tooling" below).
- python-datasource.json: JSON config containing JDBC-style URL and credentials for a
  named alias (Postgres and MySQL aliases can coexist in the same file). Gitignored —
  never commit real credentials.
- tests/: pytest suite — unit tests (mocked, always run), tests/integration/ (dockerized
  MySQL via testcontainers, auto-skips if Docker isn't available), tests/live/ (hits the
  real PoC cluster, opt-in via `pytest -m live_cluster`, skips if python-datasource.json
  is absent).

Vendor separation
- Postgres and MySQL are deliberately kept as separate script pairs rather than unified
  behind a --db-type flag. The dialects differ enough (execute_values vs executemany,
  SERIAL vs AUTO_INCREMENT, sslmode string vs ssl dict, native vs hand-rolled pooling,
  and now DDL-drift/transfer/cascade semantics that only exist on the MySQL side) that
  branching inside shared functions would add more complexity than it removes. Keep this
  pattern for any future dialect (mirror the file pair, don't merge them). Postgres has
  not been extended with the DDL/DML simulation capability described below — that was
  scoped to the active MySQL CDC PoC only; treat it as backlog if Postgres ever needs it.

Configuration model
- JSON schema per alias (Postgres):
  {
    "PSQLDS1": {
      "url": "jdbc:postgresql://host:port/dbname",
      "username": "...",
      "password": "...",
      "ssl": true|false
    }
  }
- JSON schema per alias (MySQL) — same shape, jdbc:mysql:// scheme:
  {
    "MYSQLDS1": {
      "url": "jdbc:mysql://host:port/dbname",
      "username": "...",
      "password": "...",
      "ssl": true|false
    }
  }
- parse_jdbc_url (mysql_generator/config.py for MySQL) converts JDBC URL → driver
  connection params {host, port, dbname/database}.
- ssl handling (Postgres): map ssl true → sslmode=require; false/omitted → sslmode=disable.
- ssl handling (MySQL): map ssl true → PyMySQL ssl={} (enables TLS with the default
  context; PyMySQL treats the ssl kwarg as ssl.wrap_socket()-style kwargs, NOT a
  mysql-connector-style {"ssl_mode": "REQUIRED"} dict — that shape is silently wrong
  and causes connect() to hang rather than raise, so don't reintroduce it). ssl
  false/omitted → no ssl kwarg passed.
- Prefer placing configs at repo root as "python-datasource.json" for consistency across scripts.

Aurora reader/writer gotcha (MySQL only)
- Aurora MySQL only generates binlogs on the writer; the reader has no binlog of its own
  and rejects all DDL/DML. mysql_generator/guard.py's assert_writable() checks this at
  connect time and fails fast with a clear message before any statement runs.
- The naive check is wrong: Aurora readers report @@read_only=0 (that variable reflects
  standalone-MySQL replication state, which Aurora's shared-storage architecture doesn't
  use). The actual signal is @@innodb_read_only=1. This was confirmed against the real
  cluster — don't revert to @@read_only.

Database schema and operations (Postgres — simulate_data.py)
- Table pattern: transactions_{n}
  - id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY
  - transaction_ts TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
  - amount NUMERIC(10,2) NOT NULL
  - description TEXT
- Table lifecycle helpers: create_table_if_not_exists(conn, table_name),
  reset_table(conn, table_name) → DROP then re-create.
- Record fields: amount (random float [1.00, 1000.00]), description (random 10-char
  alphanumeric). Batch inserts use psycopg2.extras.execute_values.
- Concurrency: ThreadPoolExecutor + psycopg2.pool.SimpleConnectionPool sized to
  num_workers. Batches generated lazily via generate_batches.
- simulate_production(conn, table_names, batch_size, delay): continuously inserts
  batches into random tables, sleeping between batches (Ctrl+C to stop). This is the
  ONLY simulate mode Postgres has — it does not have DDL drift, transfers, cascading
  deletes, or a changelog; those are MySQL-only (see below).

MySQL two-tier schema (mysql_generator/schema.py)
- accounts (one shared parent table, ACCOUNTS_TABLE constant): id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT
  PK, balance DECIMAL(14,2), owner_name, status, created_at.
  DELIBERATELY FROZEN from DDL drift — every transactions_{n} table references it via
  FK, and balance is load-bearing for the transfer conservation invariant. Never add it
  to a drift pool.
- transactions_{n} (N child tables, --num_tables as before): id, transaction_ts, amount,
  description, account_id BIGINT NOT NULL, status, deleted_at.
  FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE — fixed at table
  creation, NEVER part of the DDL-drift pool (the cascade relationship is the thing
  under test, not itself a variable).
- PROTECTED_TRANSACTION_COLUMNS = {id, account_id, status, deleted_at, amount,
  transaction_ts} — these can never be DROP/MODIFY/RENAME'd by drift. id/account_id are
  the PK/FK; status/deleted_at back soft-delete; amount/transaction_ts are core row
  metadata referenced by name throughout mysql_generator/workload.py (a transfer leg
  with no amount is meaningless) and matter for downstream CDC analysis. This list grew
  during implementation — amount and transaction_ts were both found missing via a real
  smoke test against the live cluster (DDL drift silently dropped them and nothing broke
  functionally, but it was clearly wrong); if you add a new column that other code
  references by name, protect it too.
  Only "description" and any column a prior ADD COLUMN drift event introduced are
  genuinely driftable.

MySQL DDL drift (mysql_generator/schema.py: DDL_OPERATIONS, run_random_ddl_drift)
- Six operations, all scoped to transactions_{n} tables only (never accounts):
  add_column, drop_column, modify_column, rename_column, add_index, drop_index.
- add_index on a TEXT/BLOB column needs an explicit key-length prefix in MySQL (error
  1170 otherwise) — ddl_add_index handles this (TEXT_BLOB_TYPES set, `col(50)` prefix).
  Found via smoke test; don't regress it.
- drop_index only ever drops indexes whose name matches the "idx_" prefix this tool
  itself uses in ddl_add_index — never PRIMARY, never the FK-required index on
  account_id. This is enforced by naming convention, not a live FK-usage check, so keep
  that prefix convention if you add more index-creating drift.
- Per-table independent scheduling: each table gets its own next-fire time, jittered
  0.5x–1.5x around --ddl_interval_seconds, re-randomized after each fire (see
  simulate_data_mysql.py: run_simulation's ddl_loop).
- run_random_ddl_drift tries ops in random order until one actually applies (e.g.
  drop_column is a no-op with nothing driftable yet) and returns the op name, or None.

Concurrency safety around DDL drift (mysql_generator/concurrency.py: QuiesceGate)
- A single global gate, not per-table: DML operations wrap in gate.dml_guard() (readers),
  a firing DDL event wraps in gate.ddl_section() (writer) — blocks new DML from starting
  and waits for in-flight DML to finish before the ALTER runs. Deliberately simple:
  migrations are rare (~minutes apart) and each DML op is a single-statement commit, so a
  brief global pause is cheap and much easier to reason about than per-table RW locks.
- mysql_generator/workload.py's insert/update/transfer re-introspect current columns
  (schema.get_columns) before building SQL rather than hardcoding "amount, description"
  — this is what lets DROP/RENAME COLUMN drift on "description" happen mid-run without
  breaking the DML layer. amount/transaction_ts don't need this because they're
  protected and always present.
- Both the DDL loop and DML workers in simulate_data_mysql.py catch pymysql.err.MySQLError
  broadly (not just OperationalError — IntegrityError from a genuine concurrent-delete
  race was seen in testing), roll back, log, and continue rather than crashing the
  worker/thread. This was a real bug found via smoke test (concurrent insert racing a
  cascading account delete raised IntegrityError, which the narrower except clause let
  propagate and silently killed a worker thread).

MySQL DML operation mix (mysql_generator/workload.py, dispatched from simulate_data_mysql.py:_dml_tick)
- Four operation types, weighted: --insert_ratio (60), --update_ratio (30),
  --delete_ratio (10), --transfer_ratio (5, supplementary — drawn from the same weighted
  pool alongside the other three, not carved out of them).
- Row selection is recency-biased (--recent_bias, default 0.85): mysql_generator/ids.py's
  IdTracker keeps a bounded in-memory deque of recent ids per table (thread-safe), primed
  from existing data via ids.seed_from_table() before --simulate starts. pick_target_id()
  samples from it most of the time, falling back to a uniform-random id-range query
  otherwise — mimics real OLTP's skew toward recently-touched rows without extra query
  load on the hot path.
- --delete_mode hard|soft (default hard). Soft delete sets status='deleted'/deleted_at
  instead of removing the row, and NEVER cascades (MySQL's ON DELETE CASCADE only fires
  on an actual DELETE statement — there is no compensating application-level cascade for
  soft deletes, by design).
- --cascade_delete_ratio (default 10): percent of hard deletes that target accounts
  instead of a single transaction row (workload.cascading_delete_account). Before issuing
  the parent DELETE, this queries every transactions_{n} table for the account's current
  child rows and logs that set as "expected_children" — MySQL's binlog only reflects the
  client's DELETE FROM accounts statement explicitly; the cascaded child-row deletes are
  only visible as row events at the storage-engine level (this cluster runs
  binlog_row_image=FULL / ROW format specifically so those events exist at all), and
  there's no way to ask MySQL afterward which children were removed.
- transfer (workload.transfer): debits one account, credits another, inserts a ledger row
  in each account's transactions_{n} table for that leg — all in one commit. This is what
  makes the balance-conservation invariant checkable: sum(accounts.balance) should be
  unchanged by any transfer, and Snowflake-side verification can recompute balances from
  the transaction rows and compare.

Ground-truth changelog (mysql_generator/changelog.py)
- Local append-only JSONL file, NOT a database table — a table would itself flow through
  the binlog as CDC noise that Artie/Openflow would need to be configured to exclude.
  Each line is written only after its operation actually committed.
- One line per committed operation for everything simulate mode does: insert, update,
  delete, soft_delete, cascade_delete (includes account_balance + expected_children),
  transfer (includes both ledger row ids + amount), ddl (includes the exact ALTER
  statement).
- Exception: the initial bulk-seed phase (--records_per_table, up to 10M rows) logs one
  "bulk_insert_batch" entry per batch (count only), not per row — logging millions of
  individual seed rows would defeat the point of batch inserts and bloat the file for a
  phase that isn't part of the CDC-mechanics signal under test.
- Accounts are the one exception to the bulk-vs-per-row split: seed_accounts logs one
  "insert" entry per account (id + balance + owner_name), even though it's technically a
  bulk-insert path, because --num_accounts is small (dozens/hundreds, not millions) and
  the balance-conservation invariant can only be verified from the changelog alone (see
  below) if each account's starting balance is actually in it.
- read_entries(path) parses a changelog back into a list of dicts — used by tests and by
  verify_snowflake.py.

Verification tooling (snowflake_verify/, verify_snowflake.py)
- Separate script/package from the MySQL generator, consistent with the vendor-separation
  principle above — this one's Snowflake-side, not MySQL-side.
- snowflake_verify/reconcile.py: pure logic, replays changelog entries into expected final
  state ({accounts: {id: {balance, deleted}}, tables: {table: {id: {amount, status,
  deleted}}}}) and diffs it against actual Snowflake rows. Fully unit-tested
  (tests/test_snowflake_reconcile.py) without needing a live Snowflake connection.
  Deliberately does NOT re-query MySQL — deleted rows can't be re-queried after the fact
  anyway, so the changelog is the only consistent source of truth for inserts, updates,
  and deletes alike (this was an explicit design decision, not an oversight).
- snowflake_verify/snow_cli.py: shells out to `snow sql -c <connection> --format json`
  rather than using snowflake-connector-python directly — this environment's Snowflake
  connection ("snowflake-cli") uses EXTERNALBROWSER (interactive SSO) auth, which the
  `snow` CLI already handles; a second Python-native driver would duplicate that auth
  setup for no benefit. Mirrors the existing project convention (see sandbox
  snowflake_dwh/DE-3834 scripts) for account-usage/cost queries.
- IMPORTANT — the Snowflake database/schema/table names in verify_snowflake.py are
  placeholders (--sf-database/--sf-schema are required CLI args, not defaulted) and
  snow_cli.py assumes the landing table mirrors MySQL's column names (id, amount,
  status, balance) exactly. Neither has been validated against real data: as of writing
  this, the Openflow ingestion track for this PoC hadn't started yet (see HANDOFF.md) and
  Artie was still being wired up via PrivateLink, so nothing has landed in Snowflake to
  verify against. Fix snow_cli.py's column-name assumptions once the actual Openflow/
  Artie landing schema is known — don't assume it matches without checking.

CLI contracts
- simulate_data_mysql.py and test_connection_mysql.py mirror the flag contracts below
  exactly (same names, defaults, and semantics), with two differences: default --config
  is still "python-datasource.json" but the default --alias is "MYSQLDS1" instead of
  "PSQLDS1".
- simulate_data.py flags:
  - --config: path to JSON config (default should be repo-root "python-datasource.json").
  - --alias: JSON alias (default "PSQLDS1").
  - --num_tables: 1..1000.
  - --start_table: ≥ 1 and ≤ num_tables; tables below start_table are skipped.
  - --records_per_table: 1000..10000000.
  - --simulate: enable continuous production mode.
  - --batch_size: default 10000.
  - --delay: sleep seconds between simulation batches.
  - --num_workers: >1 enables concurrent insert path; else sequential.
  - --reset_all: drop and recreate all processed tables.
  - --reset_tables: comma-separated table numbers to reset (e.g., "1,3,5").
- test_connection.py flags: --config, --alias (same semantics as above).
- simulate_data_mysql.py additional/changed flags beyond the Postgres set:
  - --num_accounts (default 100): rows to seed in the shared accounts table.
  - --reset_all / --reset_tables now only drop+recreate the specified transactions_{n}
    tables — accounts is left intact (it's shared infrastructure, not per-run disposable;
    only --drop_database removes it).
  - --drop_database: teardown — drops every table this tool owns (transactions_* and
    accounts) via information_schema lookup, then exits. Does NOT issue DROP DATABASE
    (avoids disturbing grants/config if the config alias ever points somewhere shared);
    dropping every owned table is practically equivalent for this ephemeral PoC schema.
  - --duration (seconds, optional): auto-stop --simulate after this long, for running
    fixed-length comparable windows (needed to compare Artie vs Openflow cost/latency on
    equivalent workloads — see below). Default: run until Ctrl+C, as before.
  - --seed (int, optional): seeds Python's random module for reproducible runs — same
    purpose as --duration, making two separate runs (e.g. one against Openflow today, one
    against Artie later once it's live) statistically comparable in shape.
  - --insert_ratio/--update_ratio/--delete_ratio/--transfer_ratio, --recent_bias,
    --delete_mode, --cascade_delete_ratio, --ddl_interval_seconds, --ddl_ops, --changelog:
    see "MySQL DML operation mix" and "MySQL DDL drift" above for semantics/defaults.

Security and secrets
- Do NOT commit real credentials. Treat example configs as placeholders; rotate if any real values were ever committed.
- Prefer environment variables or secret managers for credentials. If JSON must be used locally, keep it out of version control and add to .gitignore.
- Never interpolate untrusted inputs into SQL identifiers or queries. Table names here are generated (transactions_{n}, accounts); keep it that way. DDL drift generates its own column/index names (mysql_generator/schema.py's _random_suffix) rather than accepting external input.

Style and code conventions
- Python only; keep scripts simple and readable. Prefer functions with clear responsibilities and docstrings.
- Naming: descriptive, full words (avoid abbreviations and 1–2 letter names).
- Control flow: use guard clauses for validation; avoid deep nesting.
- Error handling: catch exceptions where there is actionable remediation or user-facing messaging; otherwise let them propagate. Exception: simulate mode's DML/DDL loops catch
  pymysql.err.MySQLError broadly and continue (see "Concurrency safety" above) — that's a
  deliberate exception to "let it propagate," not a precedent for swallowing errors
  elsewhere.
- SQL: use execute_values (Postgres) / executemany (MySQL) for batch parameterization; avoid building VALUES tuples manually.
- Logging: current scripts use print; keep consistency. If adding structured logging, do so across the codebase in one cohesive change.
- Typing: new/modified functions should add type hints. Do not over-annotate trivial locals.
- Formatting: multi-line over overly terse one-liners; wrap long lines; avoid unrelated reformatting.

Performance guidance
- Favor batch_size around 10k unless memory/latency requirements dictate otherwise.
- Set num_workers to a small integer relative to cores and database limits; pool size should match num_workers.
- Keep commits per batch to balance durability and throughput. Avoid per-row commits.
- MySQL --simulate mode operates on individual logical operations (one insert/update/
  delete/transfer/DDL event) per tick, not big batches — this is deliberate, matching
  "typical of a transactional system in production" rather than the Postgres bulk-batch
  model. --delay applies per-tick, per worker.

Path and consistency notes
- Unify default config path across scripts to "python-datasource.json" at the repo root.
- If changing defaults, update README and examples together.

Runbook examples (Postgres)
- Test connectivity:
  python3 test_connection.py --config python-datasource.json --alias PSQLDS1
- Generate data sequentially (single table):
  python3 simulate_data.py --config python-datasource.json --alias PSQLDS1 --num_tables 1 --records_per_table 10000 --batch_size 5000
- Generate data concurrently (four workers):
  python3 simulate_data.py --config python-datasource.json --alias PSQLDS1 --num_tables 4 --records_per_table 200000 --batch_size 10000 --num_workers 4
- Simulate continuous production:
  python3 simulate_data.py --config python-datasource.json --alias PSQLDS1 --num_tables 3 --records_per_table 1000 --simulate --batch_size 1000 --delay 1.5

Runbook examples (MySQL, via cdcgen.py — preferred for day-to-day use)
- Test connectivity: ./cdcgen.py check
- Bulk-seed only: ./cdcgen.py seed --num-tables 1 --records-per-table 10000 --num-accounts 100
- Full DDL+DML simulation, fixed 10-minute window, reproducible:
  ./cdcgen.py simulate --num-tables 3 --records-per-table 10000 --num-accounts 200 \
    --duration 600 --seed 42 --num-workers 4 --changelog run1_changelog.jsonl
- Reset transactions tables (accounts left intact): ./cdcgen.py reset --num-tables 3
- Teardown everything this tool owns: ./cdcgen.py teardown
- Verify Snowflake state against a changelog (once the landing schema is known):
  ./cdcgen.py verify --changelog run1_changelog.jsonl --sf-database CDC_POC --sf-schema PUBLIC --tables transactions_1,transactions_2,transactions_3
- Run tests: ./cdcgen.py selftest (unit only) / ./cdcgen.py selftest --live (+ real cluster)
- --config/--alias work before or after the subcommand; default to
  python-datasource.json / CDC_POC_MYSQL, override either position if needed.

Runbook examples (MySQL, underlying scripts directly — for flags cdcgen.py doesn't expose)
- Test connectivity (also reports writer/reader role):
  python3 test_connection_mysql.py --config python-datasource.json --alias MYSQLDS1
- Bulk-seed only (accounts + transactions_1, no simulation):
  python3 simulate_data_mysql.py --config python-datasource.json --alias MYSQLDS1 --num_tables 1 --records_per_table 10000 --num_accounts 100
- Full DDL+DML simulation, fixed 10-minute window, reproducible:
  python3 simulate_data_mysql.py --config python-datasource.json --alias MYSQLDS1 \
    --num_tables 3 --records_per_table 10000 --num_accounts 200 \
    --simulate --duration 600 --seed 42 --num_workers 4 \
    --changelog run1_changelog.jsonl
- Teardown everything this tool owns:
  python3 simulate_data_mysql.py --config python-datasource.json --alias MYSQLDS1 --drop_database
- Verify Snowflake state against a changelog (once the landing schema is known):
  python3 verify_snowflake.py --changelog run1_changelog.jsonl --sf-database CDC_POC --sf-schema PUBLIC --tables transactions_1,transactions_2,transactions_3

Testing and validation
- Run the default suite (fast, no external deps): ./venv/bin/pytest — runs unit tests
  only; tests/integration/ auto-skips without Docker (checked live: this dev laptop has
  no Docker installed, so integration tests are written-but-unvalidated here — they run
  wherever Docker is available, e.g. CI); tests/live_cluster/ is excluded by pytest.ini's
  default addopts.
- Run against the real PoC cluster explicitly: ./venv/bin/pytest -m live_cluster — skips
  itself if python-datasource.json isn't present. This actually ran clean against the
  live cdc-poc-artie2-aurora-mysql cluster during implementation (full lifecycle: schema
  creation, seed, insert/update/soft-delete/transfer/DDL-drift, then cleans up its own
  table).
- After code changes to mysql_generator/, at minimum re-run the unit suite; for changes
  touching schema.py/workload.py/concurrency.py, also do a short real --simulate run
  (small --duration, small --ddl_interval_seconds) against the live cluster and inspect
  the changelog — several real bugs (wrong Aurora read-only variable, TEXT column index
  key-length, protected-columns gaps, exception type too narrow) were only caught this
  way, not by unit tests.
- For Postgres: run test_connection.py and a small sequential insert; check row counts
  and schema.

Non-goals
- ORMs, migrations-as-a-framework, and complex schema management are out of scope — the
  DDL drift capability described above is a deliberately narrow, generator-controlled set
  of ALTERs for CDC testing, not a general migration tool.
- A unified multi-vendor CLI (single script branching on --db-type) is not targeted —
  see "Vendor separation" above. Adding more dialects means adding more script pairs,
  not branching existing ones.
- Full type-aware adaptation to arbitrary DDL drift (e.g. an INSERT auto-adjusting its
  value generation if MODIFY COLUMN changed a driftable column's type mid-run) is not
  implemented — only column *existence* is re-introspected, not type. A type-changing
  MODIFY on "description" can still cause an occasional insert/update to fail; this is
  caught by the broad MySQLError handling (logged, skipped, doesn't crash) rather than
  prevented. Treat this as acceptable graceful degradation, not a bug to silently fix by
  over-protecting more columns.
- Artie-vs-Openflow cost/performance measurement tooling (querying
  WAREHOUSE_METERING_HISTORY / Snowpipe Streaming credits) is out of scope for this repo
  — it's Snowflake-side analysis, not data generation, and belongs with the existing
  sandbox cost-analysis scripts (see snowflake_dwh/DE-3834 for reusable query patterns).
  This repo's job is producing the --seed/--duration-controlled comparable workload the
  cost analysis would run against.

Backlog candidates (if you extend the project)
- Mirror the MySQL DDL/DML simulation capability into simulate_data.py for Postgres, if a
  future PoC needs it there too (explicitly out of scope for now — see Vendor separation).
- Confirm the real Openflow/Artie Snowflake landing schema and fix snowflake_verify/
  snow_cli.py's column-name assumptions accordingly; currently unvalidated against live
  data since neither ingestion pipeline had landed anything as of implementation.
- Type-aware DDL-drift-safe value generation (see Non-goals) if the current
  graceful-degradation behavior proves too noisy in practice.
- Centralize config loading further / add .env support.
- Replace print with logging and configurable log levels.
